=== ogkm-version-tag gate (every NVIDIA citation names its tree)
crates/kf-abi/src/chanbudget.rs:10://! `ogkm: kernel_fifo_ctrl.c:317-326`) minus index `CHANNEL_GROUPS_IN_USE_PER_ENGINE` (9: what the host and every

★ UNTAGGED-CITATION BREACH — mode2_gsp_port_plan.md §0.1.
The lines above cite NVIDIA source as plain 'ogkm:', which does not say
WHICH vendored tree the claim was read from. Two are vendored and they
are different specifications:
  ogkm-580: = research_clones/ogkm-580.159.04/  (580.159.04)
              ★ the bench's own driver. Governs when the two disagree.
  ogkm-610: = research_clones/ogkm/             (610.43.02)
Re-read the claim against a tree and tag it. Three outcomes, all legitimate:
  - holds at BOTH, identically -> cite both, e.g.
        (`ogkm-610: rpc.c:10856`, `ogkm-580: :11051`)
    This is a load-bearing negative result, not a shrug: it tells the next
    reader there is no version seam here, so nobody adds a version profile
    for a struct that has none.
  - DIFFERS between the tags -> that is a SEAM. Tag it with the version it
    describes and say what the other tag does. A version-split fact is a
    profile/table entry, NEVER an `if version ==` in a logic crate.
  - the path or line MOVED -> correct it, with the tag. The whole msgq
    library is src/common/shared/msgq/ at 580 and
    src/nvidia/src/libraries/msgq/ at 610; path drift is as dangerous as
    content drift and less visible.
Line numbers belong to the tagged tree ONLY — never carry one across a tag,
even where the code is byte-identical.
If the citation says what it claims at NEITHER tag, do not quietly repoint
it: that is a phantom, and it is the most valuable thing you can find.
    ★ FAILED

